WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.800
When knitters are first introduced
to knitting machines,

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.680
they're often surprised and  somewhat
disappointed to find out that we can't

3
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.720
create kKnit and Purl stitch
combinations as easily on the machine

4
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.640
a we can by hand.

5
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.568
The default stitch for flatbed
machines is stockinette.

6
00:00:21.768 --> 00:00:24.920
Brother knitters can invest in a Garter

7
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.640
Carriage that easily produces
both knit and Purl stitches.

8
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.960
But not all of us have that luxury.

9
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:39.200
You can hand manipulate and reform
individual stitches one at a time.

10
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:45.320
This is practical for a small sections
of knitting, maybe one or two purl rows

11
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.360
here or there, but it can get a pretty
tedious for all over patterns.

12
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:55.440
Utilizing the ribber, we can
create both knit and purl stitches.

13
00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.280
Although it's similar to reforming

14
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.480
individual stitches,
you might find that using the ribber

15
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:08.000
and transferring stitches between the beds
is worth the effort that it takes.

16
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.600
A let's walk through creating this

17
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.680
a popular checkerboard stitch
pattern on the machine.

18
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.000
And decide if it's worth the extra

19
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.160
time.
Caston both beds and set your machine to "P"

20
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.720
so that the needles line up
directly across from each other.

21
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.880
It'll make it easier
to transfer the stitches.

22
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.200
Now be sure that when you arrange your

23
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.160
needles, the sets of needles
that you're transferring

24
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.400
are lternating. In other words,

25
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.960
you don't want needles to be
crashing into each other.

26
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.480
Once you hang your cast on comb,
arrange your needles in groups

27
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:50.000
of six. On the main bed, six needles in work

28
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.040
and six needles out of a work,
alternating across the bed.

29
00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.320
Set the ribber needles in the same
manner, alternating with the main bed.

30
00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:06.520
6 needles in work, 6 needles out
of work. Then knit for eight rows.

31
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:13.360
Next, transfer the ribber
needles to the main bed.And

32
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.800
the main bed needles down to the ribber.

33
00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.640
And knit eight rows.

34
00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.680
Repeat this sequence for the length

35
00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.000
of your knitting to create
the checkerboard pattern.

36
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.480
You can do the transferring of stitches
between the beds by using your double-eye

37
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.480
needle one at a time
from one bed to the other.

38
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:41.800
Or

39
00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:48.000
try using a three-prong transfer
tool to make the transfers.

40
00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.280
Remove three stitches on the tool.

41
00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:56.320
Carefully transfer the three stitches
to a second three prong tool.

42
00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:01.080
And then deposit those
stitches on the opposite bed.

43
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.960
Once you get in a rhythm,
it really isn't too difficult.

44
00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.840
I found success by first holding

45
00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.440
the first tool in my right
or my dominant hand.

46
00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:17.800
Remove the stitches,
then I switch the tool to my left hand

47
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.480
and use the second empty
tool in my right hand

48
00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.280
again to make the transfer.

49
00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.440
See what works for you.

50
00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.680
But for me, this method was a much faster

51
00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:35.280
than transferring the stitches one
by one with a double-eyed needle.

52
00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:55.680
Mistakes are going to happen. With all

53
00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:59.120
these transfers back and forth,
you're bound to drop stitches.

54
00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:00.720
Don't panic.

55
00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.680
It's always a best to pick up the dropped

56
00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:08.440
stitch from the row below and hang
the stitch on the bed where it started.

57
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:14.720
That way, you can repair the stitch
and be sure not to split the yarn.

58
00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:31.040
Then continue the transfer
with the picked up and repaired stitch.

59
00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.040
Snd you're back in business.

60
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:39.120
Another thing to note is that some brands of

61
00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:43.400
a standard gauge machines have
an accessory called a transfer carriage.

62
00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:44.680
Again,

63
00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:46.760
not all of us are a lucky enough to own

64
00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:51.080
one, but if you do, this might
be a good time to dust it off

65
00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.400
and try to create this
reversible checkerboard pattern.

66
00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.680
Can you start to imagine
more possibilities

67
00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:03.680
in mixing up the transfers
of the knit and purl stitches?

68
00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.400
Either way, it's your call. Transfer

69
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.680
the stitches,
or a walk away from the machine,

70
00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.720
pick up your hand knitting
needles and get knitting

71
00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:18.560
Happy Knitting

